Hi

I'm trying to infer parameters of a time series model with hidden states, and I would like to know if it's possible to avoid estimating the hidden states themselves.

A toy example of how I've done it so far, which includes the hidden states, is the following

data {
  int T;
  vector[T] y; // observations
  real dt; // time step for SDE
}
parameters {
  vector[T] x; // hidden states
  real a;  // parameter I'm interested in
  real sig;
  real eps;
}
model {
  for (t in 2:T)
    x[t] ~ normal(x[t-1] + dt*(a*x[t-1] - x[t-1]^3), sig*sqrt(dt));
  y ~ normal(1/(1 - exp(-x)), eps);
}

And this works quite well, but for the real model I want to use, there's not 1 hidden state variable but tens or hundreds, the observation model is a general linear mixture and aside from making the inference quite slow, I'm worried it reduces the quality of the estimates (though I'm quite new to Stan & MCMC so maybe that's unfounded). The only trick I've come up with so far is to thin the number of time points being estimated.

Can anyone offer some pointers or critiques here? Any remarks on SDEs in Stan/MCMC in general would also be welcome.

Cheers,
Marmaduke

I am trying to implement an inverse-Wishart prior for tau in the eight schools example. It's not a multivariate tau, but I just wanted to try the 1x1 case, so that I could match the results using MCMCglmm, which uses an inverse-Wishart prior.

I can get my code to run, but somehow it's not producing any results:

model.code <- "
  data {
    int<lower=0> J; // number of schools
    real y[J]; // estimated treatment effects
    real<lower=0> sigma[J]; // s.e. of effect estimates
  }
  transformed data {
    cov_matrix[1] S;
    S[1, 1] <- 0.001;
  }
  parameters {
    real mu;
    cov_matrix[1] tau;
    real eta[J];
  }
  transformed parameters {
    vector[J] theta;
    for (i in 1:J)
      theta[i] <- mu + to_vector(tau)[1]*eta[i];
  }
  model {
    tau ~ inv_wishart(0.001, S);
    eta ~ normal(0, 1);
    y ~ normal(theta, sigma);
  }"

The trouble with the inv_wishart function is that it returns a matrix. I had to figure out how to cast that 1x1 matrix into a real. The best I could do is to_vector(tau)[1], since there is no to_real.

Can someone help me figure out the best to implement the inv-Wishart prior?

Shyue-Ming

I just ended up using the inv_gamma function, as a univariate special case of inv_wishart.

So the code becomes:

model.code <- "
  data {
    int<lower=0> J; // number of schools
    real y[J]; // estimated treatment effects
    real<lower=0> sigma[J]; // s.e. of effect estimates
  }

  parameters {
    real mu;
    real<lower=0> tau;

    real eta[J];
  }
  transformed parameters {

    real theta[J];
    for (j in 1:J)
      theta[j] <- mu + tau * eta[j];
  }
  model {
    eta ~ normal(0, 1);
    tau ~ inv_gamma(0.001, 0.001);
    y ~ normal(theta, sigma);
  }"

